3. End Products, Commercial Use, and Customer Projects

3.1 You may create, sell, license, host, operate, maintain, or transfer End Products made using the Software. An End Product may be commercial, proprietary, closed source, internally operated, customer-hosted, or provided as a hosted service. Its source code may also be publicly available, subject to this License.

Permitted End Products include, without limitation, online stores, learning services, booking or reservation services, company websites, communities, document systems, monitoring services, customer-management systems, internal business systems, and SaaS products serving a specific business function.

3.2 An End Product does not become a Competitive Platform merely because it lets users manage content, configure forms, customize branding or layouts, automate workflows, create courses, manage stores or bookings, or deploy features within that End Product, provided its primary purpose remains a specific business function rather than generalized creation or deployment of arbitrary software projects.

3.3 Developers, agencies, consultants, and companies may build, customize, host, maintain, and deliver End Products to one or more customers. Providing services to multiple customers is permitted when the activity delivers End Products or genuine customer-specific development services rather than reusable templates or a Competitive Platform.

3.4 When a customer lawfully receives an End Product containing the Software, the Licensor directly grants that customer, subject to this License, a worldwide, royalty-free, non-exclusive, non-transferable, and non-sublicensable license, except as expressly permitted in this Section, to:

- use, run, host, deploy, maintain, and operate the Software solely as part of that End Product;
- make copies reasonably necessary for production, staging, testing, backup, disaster recovery, and internal operations;
- modify and extend the Software for that End Product and its successor or replacement versions;
- engage employees, contractors, hosting providers, and other service providers to perform those activities on the customer's behalf; and
- transfer the End Product and these accompanying rights to a successor or acquirer as part of a lawful transfer of the End Product or substantially all of the business or assets to which it relates.

3.5 A customer may not extract, package, or use the Software or Modified Software to create or offer a reusable template, starter product, or Competitive Platform.

3.6 A source-code delivery to a customer must include the copyright notice and a complete, unmodified copy of this License in the repository root or another reasonably prominent location.

3.7 A bona fide customer's direct license under this Section survives termination of the delivering party's license if the customer received a qualifying End Product before termination, complies with this License, and did not knowingly participate in the violation that caused termination.


4. Template Redistribution Restriction

You may not sell, license, sublicense, publish, distribute, list, package, or otherwise make the Software or Modified Software available primarily as:

- a reusable website or application template;
- a starter kit or starter project;
- boilerplate;
- a theme;
- a template package or template library;
- a template, theme, starter-kit, boilerplate, or source-code marketplace product; or
- another substantially similar reusable development product.

This restriction applies even if the Software or Modified Software is renamed, translated, restyled, reorganized, bundled, or altered where its principal value remains the reusable code, structure, design, configuration, components, or starter functionality derived from the Software.

Without separate written permission from the Licensor, You may not sell or distribute access to a repository, archive, bundle, package, or download whose primary purpose is to provide the Software as a reusable development starting point.

This Section does not prohibit:

- delivering source code as part of an End Product under Section 3;
- sharing the Software privately with persons acting solely on Your behalf;
- publishing an End Product repository as permitted by Section 6;
- maintaining a permitted development fork under Section 6; or
- distributing source code where the primary purpose and principal value of the distribution is the End Product itself rather than reuse of the Software as a template or starter product.


5. Competitive Platform Restriction

You may not use, embed, integrate, host, expose, distribute, or otherwise make the Software or Modified Software available as a Material Component of a Competitive Platform.

This includes using the Software or Modified Software as:

- a template or starter project in a generalized website or application builder;
- a reusable project in a template marketplace or project-generation catalog;
- a material reusable input, retrieval source, or project base for an AI system that generates websites, applications, APIs, or software projects for third parties;
- a material part of a no-code or low-code product for creating arbitrary or varied software projects; or
- a material part of an integrated platform whose customers can select, generate, configure, deploy, or manage generalized software projects.

The following activities are not, by themselves, operating a Competitive Platform:

- operating Your own website, application, API, or SaaS business;
- building software for Your own organization;
- creating, hosting, or maintaining End Products for customers;
- providing custom development, consulting, integration, or maintenance services;
- using an internal agency or developer tool to produce customer-specific End Products, provided customers are not offered generalized self-service project creation or deployment;
- using general-purpose IDEs, AI coding tools, source-control services, CI/CD services, cloud infrastructure, or hosting services with the Software; or
- operating a commercial SaaS product whose primary purpose is a specific business function rather than enabling customers to create or deploy arbitrary software projects.

A product does not become a Competitive Platform solely because the Licensor later adds similar business functionality to JoripSpace.

Any use prohibited by this Section requires a separate written license from the Licensor.


