6.1 You may modify any part of the Software. You are not required to publish or disclose Your modifications and may keep them private.

6.2 You retain ownership of original code and materials that You create, subject to the Licensor's rights in the Software and third-party rights. You may apply proprietary, source-available, or open-source terms to Your original additions, provided those terms do not purport to relicense the original Software, restrict rights directly granted to a customer under Section 3, or conflict with applicable third-party licenses.

6.3 You may publish the source code of an End Product, including in a public Git repository, if:

- the repository's primary purpose is to provide, operate, document, or collaborate on that End Product rather than distribute a reusable template or starter project;
- the copyright notice and a complete, unmodified copy of this License are retained;
- the repository does not state or imply that the original Software is licensed under different terms; and
- the repository is not used as a Material Component of a Competitive Platform.

6.4 You may publicly maintain a fork solely for good-faith evaluation, security review, issue reproduction, development, collaboration, or contribution, provided that You:

- retain the copyright notice and a complete, unmodified copy of this License;
- clearly identify the fork as unofficial and modified, unless it is an unmodified mirror;
- do not market, sell, monetize, list, package, or promote it as a reusable template, starter kit, or marketplace product; and
- do not use it for a Competitive Platform.

A fork primarily maintained to distribute or promote a reusable template, starter product, or Competitive Platform is not permitted regardless of how it is labeled.

6.5 If You publicly distribute the Software, Modified Software, or source code containing a substantial portion of either, or deliver such source code to a customer, You must:

- retain applicable copyright, license, and attribution notices;
- include a complete, unmodified copy of this License in the repository root or another reasonably prominent location; and
- provide a reasonable notice that the Software has been modified, such as in a changelog, README, or source-control history.

6.6 You are not required to make an End Product's source code public. You are not required to display "JoripSpace," the JoripSpace logo, a copyright notice, or a license notice in an End Product's user interface. You may remove JoripSpace branding from the user interface, but may not remove or obscure copyright or license notices from distributed source code.

